{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 340,
    "dDurationMs": 4890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Since the dawn of humanity around 100 billion\npeople have lived."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5230,
    "dDurationMs": 3510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "How many will live in the future of our species?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 8740,
    "dDurationMs": 4399,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We might hope for a trillion times that if\nwe colonize the galaxy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 13140,
    "dDurationMs": 7460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But a simple statistical argument tells us\nthat the doom of our species is much, much closer."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 25900,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In some recent episodes we’ve explored the\nanthropic principle and seen how it can be"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 30410,
    "dDurationMs": 5390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "used to explain the fact that both our planet\nand our universe seem very finely tuned to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 35800,
    "dDurationMs": 1939,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "allow the development of life."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 37739,
    "dDurationMs": 5051,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Our planet and/or universe can be rare and\nunlikely as long as there are enough other"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 42790,
    "dDurationMs": 4670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "planets and/or universes to stack the odds\nin favour of our existence."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 47460,
    "dDurationMs": 4619,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We touched on both the potential power and\npotential misuse of this principle."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 52079,
    "dDurationMs": 5500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Today we’re going to push our luck, and\nsee how this controversial idea can be used"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 57579,
    "dDurationMs": 4701,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to predict the physics of our universe, and\nalso to predict the imminent demise of the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 62280,
    "dDurationMs": 1299,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "human race."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 63579,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To use the anthropic principle properly we\nneed to use it carefully."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 67659,
    "dDurationMs": 4380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We talked about weaving in the Copernican\nprinciple which tells us that, all else being"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 72039,
    "dDurationMs": 4570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "equal, we should expect to be in a pretty\ntypical part of the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 76609,
    "dDurationMs": 3580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is seemingly the opposite notion to the\nAnthropic principle."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 80189,
    "dDurationMs": 6621,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But together we get something we can use:\nour existence selects for a particular type"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 86810,
    "dDurationMs": 5750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of potentially-rare observer-supporting environments,\nbut we should expect to find ourselves in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 92560,
    "dDurationMs": 6159,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the most typical or likely type of environment\nthat is consistent with our existence."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 98719,
    "dDurationMs": 6310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In 1987, physicist Steven Weinberg used this\nform of anthropic reasoning to estimate the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 105029,
    "dDurationMs": 3340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "value of the cosmological constant."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 108369,
    "dDurationMs": 5180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As a quick refresher: the cosmological constant\ndefines the amount of dark energy, which is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 113549,
    "dDurationMs": 3541,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the stuff causing the expansion of the universe\nto accelerate."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 117090,
    "dDurationMs": 5150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A number of theories in physics from string\ntheory to eternal inflation predict that the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 122240,
    "dDurationMs": 5189,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cosmological constant could take on different\nvalues in different universes, and its value"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 127429,
    "dDurationMs": 7331,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "could be extremely high compared to this universe\n- up to 120 orders of magnitude higher according"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 134760,
    "dDurationMs": 3699,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the crudest predictions of quantum field\ntheory."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 138459,
    "dDurationMs": 6271,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Getting a low cosmological constant is extremely\nunlikely according to these pictures."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 144730,
    "dDurationMs": 4869,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But it’s a good thing ours IS low because\notherwise our universe would have blown itself"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 149599,
    "dDurationMs": 4191,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "up too quickly for stars and life to ever\nform."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 153790,
    "dDurationMs": 4460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is a good candidate for the anthropic\nprinciple - perhaps we’re just in one of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 158250,
    "dDurationMs": 5439,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the lucky universes with a low cosmological\nconstant because where else could we be?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 163689,
    "dDurationMs": 5610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But if that’s true, then we should be in\nthe most common type of lucky universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 169299,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Under the assumption that lower cosmological\nconstants were less likely than higher ones,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 174939,
    "dDurationMs": 5341,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Steven Weinberg reasoned that our universe\nshould have the maximum cosmological constant"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 180280,
    "dDurationMs": 3340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that would allow galaxies to form - and hence\nallow our existence."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 183620,
    "dDurationMs": 6819,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He got a value that was a factor of 10 higher\nthan what would be observed 10 years later"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 190439,
    "dDurationMs": 2500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "when dark energy was discovered."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 192939,
    "dDurationMs": 5731,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However before that discovery even happened,\nWeinberg realized he’d made a mistake."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 198670,
    "dDurationMs": 5819,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He had assumed that we should be in the most\ncommon type of universe that allows our existence."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 204489,
    "dDurationMs": 6661,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He realized that we should instead assume\nthat we are the most common type of observer."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 211150,
    "dDurationMs": 5910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He refined his estimate of the cosmological\nconstant to be the value that most astronomers"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 217060,
    "dDurationMs": 4940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "across the multiverse would observe, with\na few assumptions like that the number of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 222000,
    "dDurationMs": 5249,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "astronomers in a universe is proportional\nto the amount of mass that ends up forming"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 227249,
    "dDurationMs": 1421,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "galaxies in that universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 228670,
    "dDurationMs": 7409,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Weinberg calculated that the density of dark energy\nshould most typically be observed to be around 5-10 times the density"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 236079,
    "dDurationMs": 1481,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 237560,
    "dDurationMs": 5099,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That was in the early to mid 90s, right before\ndark energy was actually discovered and found"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 242659,
    "dDurationMs": 3140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to be around three times that of matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 245799,
    "dDurationMs": 4901,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, off by a factor of 2 - which considering\nthe method, and the fact that dark energy"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 250700,
    "dDurationMs": 5129,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "could potentially have spanned many, many\norders of magnitude, is pretty amazing."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 255829,
    "dDurationMs": 6041,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Weinberg’s refinement of the anthropic principle\nto talk about observers rather than environments"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 261870,
    "dDurationMs": 6290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is essentially using the Self-Sampling Assumption,\ndefined by philosopher Nick Bostrom as follows:"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 268160,
    "dDurationMs": 5450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "“All other things equal, an observer should\nreason as if they are randomly selected from"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 273610,
    "dDurationMs": 7110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the set of all actually existent observers\n(past, present and future) in their reference class.\""
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 280720,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If a type of environment or type of universe\nproduces more observers, then they are more"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 286780,
    "dDurationMs": 2720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "likely to find themselves in that type."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 289500,
    "dDurationMs": 5420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This sort of reasoning seems useful - it enabled\na decent guess at the cosmological constant."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 294920,
    "dDurationMs": 3730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, let’s push our luck and see how far we can\ntake it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 298650,
    "dDurationMs": 6579,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Allow me to introduce you to the Doomsday\nArgument, also known as the Carter Catastrophe,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 305229,
    "dDurationMs": 2071,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "after Brandon Carter who came up with it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 307300,
    "dDurationMs": 4650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that’s the same guy who brought to popular\nattention and in fact named the anthropic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 311950,
    "dDurationMs": 1590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "principle to start with."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 313540,
    "dDurationMs": 3439,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To start, let’s try a thought experiment."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 316979,
    "dDurationMs": 1881,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Imagine you have two boxes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 318860,
    "dDurationMs": 4869,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Each contains a number of balls which are\nlabeled sequentially from 1 to however many"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 323729,
    "dDurationMs": 1660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "balls are in the box."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 325389,
    "dDurationMs": 4981,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One box contains 10 balls labeled 1-10 and\nthe other contains a thousand, labeled 1-1000."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 330370,
    "dDurationMs": 5550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You don’t know which box is which, but you\nare allowed to reach in to one box and take"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 335920,
    "dDurationMs": 1910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the first ball that you touch."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 337830,
    "dDurationMs": 5170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The boxes are set up so you can’t tell the\nnumber of balls from that action."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 343000,
    "dDurationMs": 5389,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So you look at your chosen ball at it’s\nlabeled with the number 5."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 348389,
    "dDurationMs": 1710,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Which box did you choose?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 350100,
    "dDurationMs": 3260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The one with 10 or the one with 1000 balls?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 353360,
    "dDurationMs": 3860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, probably you drew from the 10-ball box, right?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 357230,
    "dDurationMs": 5290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "After all, there was a 1-in-10 chance of getting\nthat 5 from that box, versus a 1-in-1000 chance"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 362520,
    "dDurationMs": 2209,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from the 1000-ball box."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 364729,
    "dDurationMs": 2410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, let’s try another one."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 367139,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s say there are two possibilities for\nthe future of humanity: in one, we’re destined"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 372479,
    "dDurationMs": 4310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to spread throughout the Milky Way and forge\na galactic civilization that last a million"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 376789,
    "dDurationMs": 5410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of years across a million star system, and\nultimately gives rise to a hundred trillion"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 382199,
    "dDurationMs": 4571,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "trillion individual lives - 10^20."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 386770,
    "dDurationMs": 6530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the second scenario, humanity never leaves\nthe Earth and only manages to survive to produce"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 393300,
    "dDurationMs": 5230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "about the same number of people that have\nalready lived - so another 100 billion."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 398530,
    "dDurationMs": 1440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or 10^11."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 399970,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ll call these the doom-late and doom-soon\nscenarios respectively."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 406270,
    "dDurationMs": 5739,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s apply the self-sampling assumption\nand imagine that you could have been any one"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 412009,
    "dDurationMs": 3741,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the past and future humans of our species."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 415750,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We can imagine that the two scenarios are\nour two boxes - the doom-late box contains"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 422270,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "10^20 lives and doom-soon contains 10^11."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 427470,
    "dDurationMs": 4990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Think of yourself as the numbered ball pulled\nfrom one of these, and the number painted"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 432460,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on you is your birth rank, which for you is\naround 100 billion."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 437900,
    "dDurationMs": 5540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So which is more likely - that your birth\nrank happens to be somewhere around the mid-point"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 443440,
    "dDurationMs": 1820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the species lifespan?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 445260,
    "dDurationMs": 5990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or that you in the first 100 billion out of\na hundred trillion trillion - or in the first"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 451250,
    "dDurationMs": 4550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "0.000001%?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 455800,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The same logic that led you to favor the 10-ball\nbox upon drawing a 5 seems to say you should"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 461640,
    "dDurationMs": 3260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "favour the doom-soon box here also."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 464900,
    "dDurationMs": 5590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s the scenario in which humanity only\nhas about as much time left as has already"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 470490,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "past."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 471490,
    "dDurationMs": 2000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is the doomsday argument."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 473490,
    "dDurationMs": 4810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In it, our mere observation of our existence\nsuggests we’re already a good fraction of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 478300,
    "dDurationMs": 2950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the way through our species existence."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 481250,
    "dDurationMs": 4479,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact we can use statistics to estimate\nthe probability of our species lasting a certain"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 485729,
    "dDurationMs": 1791,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "amount of time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 487520,
    "dDurationMs": 5179,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Estimates vary, but a simple doomsday calculation\ngive us 50-50 odds of making it through the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 492699,
    "dDurationMs": 1960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "next couple of centuries."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 494659,
    "dDurationMs": 3290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If all of this sounds fishy, you’re not\nalone."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 497949,
    "dDurationMs": 4901,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are many proposed refutations of the\ndoomsday argument - more than we can go into"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 502850,
    "dDurationMs": 5319,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- but it’s worth covering some that get\nat how the anthropic principle is interpreted,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 508169,
    "dDurationMs": 7631,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in particular through the self-sampling assumption\nand the definition of your reference class."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 515800,
    "dDurationMs": 3850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Choosing your reference class depends on the\nquestion you’re asking."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 519650,
    "dDurationMs": 4820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In Steven Weinberg’s case the question was\n“what sort of cosmological constant is the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 524470,
    "dDurationMs": 5620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "typical astronomer likely to observe - so\nreference class was an astronomer - or really"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 530090,
    "dDurationMs": 4120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "any observer capable of measuring the cosmological\nconstant."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 534210,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the doomsday argument, the assumption is\nthat our “reference class” is all past"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 539170,
    "dDurationMs": 1870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and future humans."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 541040,
    "dDurationMs": 2410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But is that the right reference class?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 543450,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, if you define our reference class\nas all observers in our past and future evolutionary"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 549650,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "chain, then we could still be in the middle\nof that chain but have millions more years"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 554210,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ahead of us."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 555210,
    "dDurationMs": 6250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Then it all depends on whether you count all\nliving things, or somewhat conscious non-human"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 561460,
    "dDurationMs": 3260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "animals, or things with a prefrontal cortex."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 564720,
    "dDurationMs": 3950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It also depends on whether we count all of\nour descendents as “human”."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 568670,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I for one welcome the doomsday if it means\ngraduating out of our reference class into"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 574190,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "some sort of cybernetic, gene-spliced ultra-human."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 577710,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In general, it’s the fuzziness of this notion\nof reference class that makes this idea seem"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 583650,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "far less mathematically precise - far more\nfishy - than the question of picking balls"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 589450,
    "dDurationMs": 1950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from boxes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 591400,
    "dDurationMs": 4931,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Another issue is that the doomsday argument\nis assigning some sort of reality to the two"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 596331,
    "dDurationMs": 6799,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "scenarios - doom-early and doom-late are both thought\nof as existent, so that you can be randomly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 603130,
    "dDurationMs": 4070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "plucked from one - in the same way the two\nboxes exist."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 607200,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But presumably only one is true and you weren’t\nrandomly plucked from it - you just are what"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 613200,
    "dDurationMs": 5870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you are - a being with a mental experience\nconsistent with the 100-billionth entity born"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 619070,
    "dDurationMs": 4450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into a civilization that may or may not end\nsoon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 623520,
    "dDurationMs": 6760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Speaking of which, what about this idea that\nyour reference class is exactly you?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 630280,
    "dDurationMs": 4941,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or, rather, all that you know is that you’re\nan entitity that is having your current a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 635221,
    "dDurationMs": 1789,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mental experience?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 637010,
    "dDurationMs": 5910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is the definition invoked in the simulation\nhypothesis and the Boltzmann brain scenarios."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 642920,
    "dDurationMs": 5990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ve covered both of these, but in short,\nwe can come up with much more common ways"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 648910,
    "dDurationMs": 7720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that your current self exists besides evolving\non a rare planet in a rare universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 656630,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, you could be one of countless\nsimulations of yourself in some far-future"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 661390,
    "dDurationMs": 4470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "experiment to recreate the past - an ancestor\nsimulation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 665860,
    "dDurationMs": 6430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or you could be a momentary entropy fluctuation\nthat caused a brain with exactly your current"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 672290,
    "dDurationMs": 6050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mental experience and memories to pop into\nthe high-entropy post-heat-death void - that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 678340,
    "dDurationMs": 2330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one is a a Boltzann brain."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 680670,
    "dDurationMs": 3950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As you can see, anthropic reasoning can lead\nto powerful predictions, like the value of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 684620,
    "dDurationMs": 4590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the cosmological constant, it can lead to\ndubious but worrying predictions like the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 689210,
    "dDurationMs": 5020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "doomsday argument, and it can lead to some\npretty absurd ideas like the Boltzmann brain."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 694230,
    "dDurationMs": 3400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s try our own little exercise in anthropic\nreasoning."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 697630,
    "dDurationMs": 5930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Here’s a challenge question for you: Your\nbirth rank is 100 billion."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 703560,
    "dDurationMs": 5990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Assuming the doomsday argument are valid,\nwhat are the chances that humanity will last"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 709550,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "until the year 3000?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 713270,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Assuming the current rate of population increase\nslows dramatically so it’s doubling once"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 718670,
    "dDurationMs": 5581,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "every hundred years over that period Email\nyour answer to pbsspacetime@gmail.com with"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 724251,
    "dDurationMs": 4839,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the subject line Doomsday Challenge within\n2 weeks of release of this episode."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 729090,
    "dDurationMs": 2930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ll filter by subject name so check your spelling."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 732020,
    "dDurationMs": 4310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Best answers get a pick from the Space Time\nmerch store."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 736330,
    "dDurationMs": 5210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Whether or not doom is coming soon or late,\nhere’s a positive spin on the whole doomsday"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 741540,
    "dDurationMs": 7370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "argument: the chances of us being in the final\ngeneration of our civilization are also pretty"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 748910,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tiny."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 749910,
    "dDurationMs": 4290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Which means you’ll be around for next week’s\nepisode of space time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 754200,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Our Patreon contributors make a huge difference\n- ensuring we'll be able to keep making and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 760080,
    "dDurationMs": 5450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "expanding Space Time until doomsday - whether\nit's soon or late."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 765530,
    "dDurationMs": 5530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And today I wanted to give a huge shoutout\nthank you to John S, who's one of our Big"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 771060,
    "dDurationMs": 1070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Bang contibutors."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 772130,
    "dDurationMs": 5860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "John, you are in a reference class of your\nown: reference class supremely awesome."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 777990,
    "dDurationMs": 5921,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As a special thanks we pulled a few strings\nand got you moved over to the doom-never box"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 783911,
    "dDurationMs": 5959,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": ". If anyone wishes to join John S in reference\nclass awesome, any level of contribution is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 789870,
    "dDurationMs": 1640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a big help."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 791510,
    "dDurationMs": 6050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And any level - even $2 - also gets you access\nto our new Space Time discord channel, where"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 797560,
    "dDurationMs": 5550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at this very moment brilliant and curious\nindividuals are discussing the future of humanity,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 803110,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the nature of space and time, the weirdness\nof quantum mechanics - you name it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 807950,
    "dDurationMs": 4040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And don't forget with the holidays coming\nup, to check out our merch store."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 811990,
    "dDurationMs": 4510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, so our last episode was a journal club\nlooking at a study that claimed to have evidence"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 816500,
    "dDurationMs": 3260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that the universe is not infinite after all."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 819760,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let's start with a question from the discord:\nHabunelahack asks if it's ever be possible"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 825220,
    "dDurationMs": 6270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to measure the curvature of space to get an\nestimate on the size of the entire universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 831490,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, the answer is maybe: if the universe is closed\nand has positive curvature then measuring"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 837730,
    "dDurationMs": 4900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that curvature absolutely will give us the\ntotal volume of the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 842630,
    "dDurationMs": 4330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If it turns out to the universe is negatively curved then\nwell it's infinite."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 846960,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On the other hand, if we keep finding geometric\nflatness we'll never actually know whether"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 853240,
    "dDurationMs": 5380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it's finite or infinite because it may be\nthat there's a slight positive or negative"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 858620,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "curvature just beyond the sensitivity of our\nmeasurement."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 862780,
    "dDurationMs": 6490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Daniel M. and Sam Harper ask whether a universe\ncan transition between different geometries."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 869270,
    "dDurationMs": 4759,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In a universe like ours where matter and energy\nis evenly distributed on the largest scales"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 874029,
    "dDurationMs": 5091,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the curvature does\nchanges but the global geometry does not."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 879120,
    "dDurationMs": 5450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A flat or hyperbolic universe remains flat\nor hyperbolic respectively and so remains"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 884570,
    "dDurationMs": 6150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "infinite, while a positively curved universe\nremains positively curved and therefore finite."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 890720,
    "dDurationMs": 4990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That said, patchs of the universe CAN change\nin curvature depending on the behavior of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 895710,
    "dDurationMs": 1800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "matter in those patches."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 897510,
    "dDurationMs": 5910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The most obvious example is that regular gravitational\nfields around stars and galaxies can be positively"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 903420,
    "dDurationMs": 4250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "curved patches in a flat or hyperbolic universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 907670,
    "dDurationMs": 4810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That means it's also possible to produce a\nclosed, positively curved bubble universe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 912480,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "within an infinite inflating multiverse."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 916680,
    "dDurationMs": 6650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One commenter, O.N., pointed out that an infinite\nuniverse is technically possible within a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 923330,
    "dDurationMs": 3570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bubble that otherwise looks finite from the\noutside."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 926900,
    "dDurationMs": 6350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, that's something I need to dig into deeper\nbefore I comment on it - but it sounded legit"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 933250,
    "dDurationMs": 4110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in a deeply, abstractly theoretical sort of\nway."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 937360,
    "dDurationMs": 4580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Rei Spring points out that there are flat\ngeometries that are not infinite, but rather"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 941940,
    "dDurationMs": 5980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "closed - for example the 3-torus embedded which is like a donut, but with more dimensions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 947920,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now the non-brain-breaking analogy is a 2-torus\nlike in the game Asteroids, or PacMan"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 953760,
    "dDurationMs": 3250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "where opposite walls connect\nto each other."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 957040,
    "dDurationMs": 8400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These are basically flat mappings of a donut. And there are weirder, multiply connected finite space times that are also flat."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 965440,
    "dDurationMs": 5500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now while these are valid geometries for spacetimes\nthat can be constructed within Einstein's"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 970960,
    "dDurationMs": 2400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "general relativity, it's not clear"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 973360,
    "dDurationMs": 3330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "how such a universe\nwould form, and certainly forming a 3-torus"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 976690,
    "dDurationMs": 4590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as a bubble out of eternal inflation might\nnot be possible."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 981280,
    "dDurationMs": 6710,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Ni999 dropped some wisdom from Ethan Seigel,\nwhose excellent Forbes article on this closed universe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 988000,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "paper emphasized a point that we mentioned\nonly in passing that is worth repeating:"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 993520,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that is that the baryon acoustic oscillation signatures seen in galaxy clusters"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 999920,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are an independent measure of curvature, and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1003840,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "these point to a much flatter universe than\nimplied by this new result from the CMB gravitational lensing."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1009440,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In short, there's still significantly more\nevidence in favour of flatness than positive curvature."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1016480,
    "dDurationMs": 5410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Last time in an attempt to be \"funny\" I dissed\nenglish weather."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1021890,
    "dDurationMs": 6590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Shaun Humphreys kindly took the time to pen\nan extensive treatise on why the British climate"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1028480,
    "dDurationMs": 2270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is really optimal for humans."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1030750,
    "dDurationMs": 2500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I stand thoroughly corrected, Shaun."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1033250,
    "dDurationMs": 4350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Coming from Australia, where it swings between\n5000 degree heat and raining ... spiders,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1037600,
    "dDurationMs": 4750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I do envy the consistency of your 17 different\ntypes of drizzle."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1042350,
    "dDurationMs": 6539,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Also, thank you for Douglas Adams - and thank god most people got the hitchhikers reference in that last"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1048889,
    "dDurationMs": 4951,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "episode - you really are the hoopiest froods on the\ninternet."
    } ]
  } ]
}
